WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.064
Acceleration support determines whether local AI feels interactive or merely possible.

00:00:05.064 --> 00:00:11.343
NVIDIA hardware commonly uses CUDA, a mature compute platform supported by many AI runtimes.

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:21.674
AMD workflows may use ROCm where the operating system and GPU are supported, while Vulkan backends can provide broader cross-vendor acceleration for compatible tools.

00:00:21.674 --> 00:00:30.789
Support varies by model, driver, runtime build, and operating system, so a GPU brand alone does not guarantee a working path.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:34.232
Think of the stack as a chain.

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:38.486
The operating system recognizes the GPU through its driver.

00:00:38.486 --> 00:00:43.145
The runtime must be compiled for a compatible acceleration backend.

00:00:43.145 --> 00:00:49.829
That backend must support the GPU architecture and the numerical formats used by the model.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:59.755
If any link is incompatible, the application may fail to load, silently run on the CPU, or offload only part of the workload.

00:00:59.755 --> 00:01:08.060
Confirm acceleration through runtime logs and GPU utilization rather than assuming that installation success means the GPU is active.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:17.580
GPU offload places model layers or operations on the graphics processor while retaining the remainder in system memory and on the CPU.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.644
Full offload is usually faster when the model fits in VRAM.

00:01:22.644 --> 00:01:29.734
Partial offload can make a larger model usable, but data movement and CPU computation reduce speed.

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:37.633
CPU-only inference remains a valuable fallback for troubleshooting, embeddings, and smaller models, though interactive generation is typically slower.

00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:39.456
Version discipline matters.

00:01:39.456 --> 00:01:44.115
Record the known-good GPU driver, acceleration runtime, and application build.

00:01:44.115 --> 00:01:47.559
Do not update every layer at once.

00:01:47.559 --> 00:01:54.648
When a change is necessary, verify one representative model before modifying the rest of the environment.

00:01:54.648 --> 00:02:04.169
A slightly older stable stack that consistently uses acceleration is more useful than a newer combination whose compatibility has not been proven.
